      "transcript_text": "Let us move on to our next topic. We are now going to cover different forms into which a business is organized. We will start our discussion with sole proprietorship and partnership kinds of organizations. We will start with sole proprietorship. A sole proprietorship as the name implies is a business owned by one person. This form of business is suitable when the business is small and it is not expected to grow much. For example, if you are a freelancer, you are essentially a sole proprietor. You might have several questions on your mind. What is the owner called in the sole proprietorship kind of a business? The owner is called as the proprietor. How many owners are there in a sole proprietorship kind of a business? The answer is one owner because it is sole proprietorship. It is being run by the sole person who is the owner of the owner. The sole proprietorship kind of business. Yet another question which you might have on your mind is, is there any specific law in India to regulate sole proprietorship kind of a business? The answer is no. There is no specific law to regulate sole proprietorship kind of a business. Now let us talk about partnership. Partnership is also called as general partnership. Here two or more persons can come together and form a partnership kind of a business. The owners of a partnership kind of a business, they often know each other intimately. Now the question is, what are owners called as? Owners are called as partners. How many owners or partners are there in a partnership kind of a business? A partnership kind of a business cannot have less than two partners and it cannot have more than 50 partners. Now the question is, is there a specific law to regulate a partnership kind of a business in India? The answer is yes. General partnership in India is regulated under the Indian Partnership Act 1932. Now let us talk about features of sole proprietorship and partnership kinds of businesses. The first feature is, these businesses have unlimited liability, which means that the owners, which means the proprietor and the partners have unlimited liability, which means that the liability or the of the owners is not just limited to the amount of wealth they have contributed at the time of starting the business. But their personal wealth is also at stake, should the business owe some money to somebody else and if the business is unable to pay. Put differently, your personal wealth can be seized. If your business is unable to repay the amount that they have the business has borrowed. Another important feature of a proprietorship and a partnership kind of a business is that these businesses have limited life. Why? The answer is simple. These businesses come to an end if the sole proprietor or any of the partners dies. Right? Thank you.",
